From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:19:37 +0000
Subject: Build the WebAssembly interpreter on Android

V8 contains a WebAssembly interpreter that runs modules without
generating machine code. Its build files accept it only on Windows,
Linux, macOS and iOS, so v8_enable_drumbrake fails the build for
Android.

Allow Android on 64-bit x64 and arm64, the CPUs the interpreter already
supports elsewhere. On Android the interpreter checks memory bounds in
its instruction handlers instead of relying on guard pages, which is
already the default for platforms other than desktop and iOS. Android
builds compile the snapshot in a Linux host toolchain, so use the same
bounds-checked mode there too; otherwise mksnapshot and the device V8
would disagree on the list of builtins.

Build arguments apply to every toolchain. An Android build also
configures 32-bit secondary toolchains, which have no pointer
compression and cannot build the interpreter. Leave the interpreter out
of those toolchains instead of failing, so a single build argument works
for the whole build.

The interpreter sources had not followed some V8 changes and did not
compile: TrustedManaged::raw() now takes a DisallowGarbageCollection
scope, TrustedCast only accepts trusted object types, and the
SharedFunctionInfo flag bits moved into SharedFunctionInfo::Flags. Use
unsafe_raw(), Cast for objects inside the sandbox and the new flag
names. The generated code is the same.

License: GPL-2.0-or-later - https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-2.0-or-later.html
